LEG 4  THE CAST BOUNDARY -- measured, not decided
==========================================================================
  FLOATPOLICY.md 3.3 records the float-to-int boundary UNSETTLED.
  The eleven geometry sites convert a double to a 16-bit int at a
  call boundary.  Four hypotheses; this leg reports the distance
  between them.  NONE of them is asserted to be the right one.

  hypothesis                       values     differ   %
  cast=chop  castsrc=ext              55856          0    0.000%
  cast=chop  castsrc=f64              55856        111    0.199%
  cast=near  castsrc=ext              55856       7867   14.084%
  cast=near  castsrc=f64              55856       7857   14.067%

  per field, chop/ext vs near/ext:
    orb_orient         0 differing
    orb_seed        2708 differing
    tilt            1883 differing
    orb_tilt        1803 differing
    orb_ecc         1473 differing
    ray                0 differing
    orb_ray            0 differing
    ring               0 differing
  [PASS] the cast boundary reaches exactly four of the eight fields   reached: orb_ecc, orb_seed, orb_tilt, tilt

  orb_orient's draw takes an INTEGER argument; ray, orb_ray and
  ring are RECOMPUTED in phases F and G from zrandom(100) and
  random(3)/random(5)/random(2), all integer arguments.  So four
  of the eight fields cannot depend on the cast boundary at all,
  and the measurement above confirms it rather than assuming it.
  [PASS] chop and round-to-nearest are DISTINGUISHABLE in geometry   7867 of 55856 values move

  castsrc (live 80-bit value vs its binary64 rounding) moves
  111 of 55856 values.  FLOATPOLICY.md 3.3 says the engine cannot
  currently truncate an unstored extended value at all; this is
  the size of that gap for geometry specifically.

